A class is a user-defined blueprint or template that defines attributes (fields) and behaviour (methods). An object is a runtime instance of a class allocated in heap memory.

Example:
  class Car {           // class = blueprint
      String colour;
      void drive() { System.out.println("Driving"); }
  }
  Car myCar = new Car(); // object = instance
  myCar.colour = "Red";
  myCar.drive();

Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that organises software design around objects — entities that combine state (data/fields) and behaviour (methods). Java is a purely OOP language (except for primitive types).

1. ENCAPSULATION
Binding data and methods into a class and restricting access via access modifiers.
Java: private fields accessed through public getters/setters.
Example:
  class BankAccount {
      private double balance;   // hidden
      public double getBalance() { return balance; }
      public void deposit(double amt) { if (amt > 0) balance += amt; }
  }

2. ABSTRACTION
Showing only essential functionality; hiding implementation details.
Java: achieved via abstract classes and interfaces.
  abstract class Shape {
      abstract double area();  // what it does, not how
  }
  class Circle extends Shape {
      double r;
      Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }
      double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }
  }

3. INHERITANCE
A class (subclass) acquires properties of another class (superclass) using 'extends'.
Promotes reuse and 'is-a' relationship.
  class Animal {
      void breathe() { System.out.println("Breathing"); }
  }
  class Dog extends Animal {
      void bark() { System.out.println("Woof!"); }
  }
  Dog d = new Dog();
  d.breathe(); // inherited
  d.bark();    // own method

4. POLYMORPHISM
a) Compile-time (method overloading): same method name, different parameter lists.
  class MathUtils {
      int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
      double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
  }
b) Runtime (method overriding): subclass provides own implementation.
  class Animal { void sound() { System.out.println("..."); } }
  class Dog extends Animal { void sound() { System.out.println("Woof"); } }
  Animal a = new Dog(); a.sound(); // prints: Woof (dynamic binding)

OOP Benefits: modularity, reusability, maintainability, real-world modelling.

COMPARISON TABLE:
Aspect | OOP | Procedural
Focus | Objects (data + methods) | Functions acting on data
Data security | Encapsulation protects data | Global/shared data, less secure
Code reuse | Inheritance, polymorphism | Function calls, macros
Design approach | Bottom-up | Top-down
Maintainability | Easier (localised changes) | Harder for large programs
Examples | Java, C++, Python | C, Pascal, FORTRAN

SAME PROBLEM — calculating area of a rectangle:

Procedural (C style):
  float length = 5.0, width = 3.0;
  float area(float l, float w) { return l * w; }
  void main() { printf("%f", area(length, width)); }

OOP (Java):
  class Rectangle {
      private float length, width;
      Rectangle(float l, float w) { length = l; width = w; }
      float area() { return length * width; }
  }
  Rectangle r = new Rectangle(5, 3);
  System.out.println(r.area());

In OOP, data and method are inside the object. In procedural, they are separate.

OBJECT-ORIENTED MODELLING
OO modelling identifies objects, classes, attributes, methods, and relationships in a system to create a software design.

Steps:
1. Identify objects: Person, Student, Faculty, Department, Course.
2. Define attributes and methods for each class.
3. Identify relationships: inheritance (IS-A), association (HAS-A).

CLASS HIERARCHY (University System):

  class Person {               // General (Generalisation)
      String name, id;
      void display() { ... }
  }
  class Student extends Person {   // Specialisation
      int rollNo;
      double gpa;
      void enroll(Course c) { ... }
  }
  class Faculty extends Person {   // Specialisation
      String designation;
      void teach(Course c) { ... }
  }
  class Department {
      String deptName;
      List<Faculty> staff;         // Association (HAS-A)
      List<Course> courses;
  }
  class Course {
      String courseName, code;
      Faculty instructor;
      List<Student> enrolled;
  }

RELATIONSHIPS:
- IS-A: Student IS-A Person, Faculty IS-A Person (Inheritance/Generalisation)
- HAS-A: Department HAS Faculty (Composition/Association)
- Generalisation: Person generalises Student and Faculty
- Specialisation: Student and Faculty specialise Person

Benefit: Reuse Person attributes in Student/Faculty; changes to Person propagate automatically.

