An inner class is a class defined within another class. It can access the private members of the outer class.

Types:
1. Regular inner class: Non-static class inside another class.
2. Static nested class: Static class inside another class (cannot access outer instance members directly).
3. Local inner class: Defined inside a method.
4. Anonymous inner class: Class without a name, defined and instantiated at once (commonly for interface/abstract class implementation).

Example (regular inner class):
class Outer {
    private int x = 10;
    class Inner {
        void display() {
            System.out.println("x = " + x); // accesses outer private member
        }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Outer o = new Outer();
        Outer.Inner i = o.new Inner();
        i.display(); // Output: x = 10
    }
}

