Extending Thread:
  class MyThread extends Thread {
      public void run() { /* task */ }
  }
  MyThread t = new MyThread();
  t.start();

Implementing Runnable:
  class MyTask implements Runnable {
      public void run() { /* task */ }
  }
  Thread t = new Thread(new MyTask());
  t.start();

Differences:
1. Inheritance: Extending Thread uses up Java's single inheritance. Implementing Runnable leaves the class free to extend another class.
2. Reusability: Runnable separates task from thread control — same Runnable can be passed to multiple Thread objects or thread pools.
3. OOP: Runnable follows composition over inheritance principle.
4. Preferred: Implementing Runnable is preferred for flexibility and thread pool compatibility (ExecutorService accepts Runnable).

